DA-004 — Scene 09 — The New Sequence — Approved Draft v1.2

The pause lasted long enough for Eli to hear the ventilation above them.

Then two knocks came from beyond the closed staff door.

Close together.

Eli kept the camera pointed at the door.

Martin did not move.

For several seconds neither of them spoke. The corridor continued around them as it had all night: low mechanical vibration through the wall, air through the ceiling vents, a faint plumbing sound somewhere farther along the floor.

Nothing else followed.

Eli looked at Martin.

Martin was staring at the staff door.

“You heard all three.”

It was not a question.

Martin nodded once.

“From there?”

“That’s where it seemed.”

Eli adjusted his grip on the camera. “Same place for you?”

Martin glanced at him. “Door side.”

“Me too.”

Neither of them said exactly.

Eli moved closer to the boundary, stopping short of the handle. The door was the same one they had checked during the earlier source hunt: staff access, closed to them, no window, no reason to assume the corridor continued directly behind it in the direction the sound seemed to suggest.

He aimed the camera at the frame and then down at the threshold.

“No prompt,” he said quietly. “We didn’t say anything before that.”

Martin looked at him.

“I’m logging it.”

“Then log what happened.”

Eli nodded.

“One knock at the end of the last section. Pause. Two more. Both of us facing the same door.”

Martin’s eyes shifted to the camera.

“Sounded like the same door.”

Eli corrected himself. “Sounded like it came from this side.”

Martin nodded.

They waited.

Nothing repeated.

A minute ago Eli had been trying to determine whether the voices were coming from a guest room, the service passage, or somewhere he could not see. Martin had been following the knock pattern from the opposite direction. Now both trails had ended at the same locked boundary, and the only thing they shared was the last sequence.

That made the moment stronger.

It did not make the location cleaner.

Eli stepped to one side of the doorway and listened with his head turned slightly, trying to separate whatever came through the door from whatever travelled through the adjacent wall. Martin crouched and looked at the gap beneath it.

“Anything?”

Martin shook his head.

Eli moved to the other side. The camera microphone would get the impacts if they were loud enough. It would not preserve what the corridor had sounded like around them or how sharply the last two knocks had seemed to sit against everything else.

He touched the painted wall beside the frame with two fingers.

No vibration he could distinguish.

“What are you doing?” Eli asked.

“Looking.”

“For what?”

“Light. Feet. Anything moving.”

There was a thin dark line beneath the door and nothing more.

Martin stood, tried the handle, and released it when it did not move.

“Locked.”

“We knew that.”

“I know.”

He pressed his ear near the door without touching it. After several seconds he stepped back.

“Nothing.”

“Could be a service corridor.”

“Could be a room.”

“Could connect back toward the elevators.”

“Could do a lot of things.”

Eli looked along the wall.

“We don’t know.”

“No.”

Eli lowered the camera slightly.

They had reached the same problem that had stopped them earlier. The hotel did not belong to them. A staff door was not permission to enter because something interesting had happened behind it. They had no floor plan, no staff movement log, no access to whatever mechanical spaces might lie beyond the wall.

“We go back.”

Martin did not answer.

Eli turned toward him.

At the start of the night, Martin would already have been walking.

Now he was still looking at the door.

“Dad.”

Martin raised one hand.

“Wait.”

Eli stopped.

They listened again.

Ten seconds.

Twenty.

Nothing.

Somewhere well outside their immediate section, a door shut. The sound came through the corridor as a dull impact without any useful direction.

Martin looked toward it, then back at the staff door.

“That wasn’t it.”

“I know.”

Thirty seconds.

The silence after the shared cadence had lasted long enough that Eli could feel the adrenaline beginning to drain from his hands. His fingers were stiff around the camera grip.

Martin stepped closer to the door.

Eli watched him place two fingers against the painted surface.

Not his ear.

His knuckles.

Eli knew what he was about to do before Martin moved.

“Dad.”

Martin looked back.

Eli could have stopped him.

Instead he kept the camera raised.

Martin turned to the door.

He knocked once.

The sound was harder and flatter than the ones they had heard.

He waited.

Then he knocked twice.

Eli felt the old rhythm before he consciously counted it.

One.

Then two.

Martin dropped his hand.

Neither spoke.

The gesture looked different coming from him than it ever had in Eli’s memory. There was no ghost story attached to it now. No voice lowered for a child. No performance.

Just Martin standing in a hotel corridor after two hours of insisting that a pattern was not a source, a resemblance was not a cause, and hearing something did not make it communication.

Eli looked at the camera screen.

The red recording indicator remained steady.

He looked back at the door.

Nothing followed.

Five seconds.

Ten.

Martin shifted his weight.

At fifteen seconds he rubbed the side of his thumb against his forefinger. Eli knew the gesture as one of his father’s old waiting habits.

Twenty seconds.

The longer nothing happened, the smaller the knock-back became.

A man tapping on a staff door in the middle of the night.

Eli almost lowered the camera.

Martin exhaled through his nose.

“Well.”

Eli looked at him.

Martin gave the slightest shake of his head.

There it was.

Not relief exactly.

Something close enough.

Eli lowered the camera a few inches.

One knock sounded behind them.

Both turned.

The impact seemed to come from behind them.

Or the wall beside it.

Or somewhere beyond the bend.

Eli raised the camera again.

The pause followed.

Martin took one step away from the staff door.

Two knocks.

Close together.

Eli checked the camera screen.

Still recording.

Martin turned fully toward the corridor.

“That was behind us.”

“I thought farther left.”

Martin pointed toward the bend. “No. Back toward the guest hall.”

“I heard it closer to the wall.”

“That wall goes back toward the guest hall.”

“You don’t know what’s behind it.”

Martin stopped.

Neither interpretation gave them a source.

Eli moved toward the bend and aimed the camera down the open corridor.

Empty.

Martin came beside him.

“Anybody there?”

Eli looked at him.

Martin had called out, not Eli.

No one answered.

They waited.

A faint mechanical hum continued somewhere beyond the wall.

Martin walked to the first accessible guest-side junction and stopped. Eli followed, keeping him in frame.

“No staff door here,” Eli said.

“I can see that.”

“Guest rooms start there.”

“I know.”

Martin listened at the bend.

Nothing moved.

Eli swept the camera slowly across the corridor. Closed doors. Wall vents. Carpet. The service-side wall. No visible person.

“Which direction did you hear?”

Martin pointed.

“Down there.”

Eli pointed a little closer to the wall beside them.

“I had it here.”

“That’s not the same place.”

“No.”

Martin looked from Eli’s hand to the corridor.

For the first time since the new sequence, something in his face eased.

Not because the event had become ordinary.

Because they disagreed.

The new impacts left them with the same problem they had already faced: both men had heard them, and neither could place them precisely.

Eli stepped to the wall and listened.

Nothing.

He checked the nearest unlocked public door.

A linen alcove.

Empty shelves on one side, folded towels on the other.

No person.

Martin looked past him.

“Does that connect?”

“No.”

“You sure?”

Eli angled the camera inside.

“Ends right there.”

Martin moved farther down the corridor.

Eli followed.

“Dad.”

Martin stopped at another junction.

“What?”

“We’re done.”

Martin looked back toward the staff door.

“You heard that.”

“Yes.”

“After I knocked.”

“Yes.”

“From somewhere else.”

“Somewhere we couldn’t see.”

Martin stared at him.

Eli kept the camera lowered between them now.

“You know what I mean.”

Eli looked toward the corridor instead of answering.

Martin waited, then looked away.

For most of the night, Eli had been the one trying to close that distance.

He had wanted Martin to move from I heard it to I believe you, from I can’t explain it to something Eli could carry home as confirmation.

Now Martin was trying to move one step past the event himself.

Eli did not let him.

“We don’t know what’s behind that door. Somebody could be awake up here. We’ve spent half the night hearing doors through walls, and we can’t even agree where those knocks came from.”

Martin pointed toward the corridor.

“So we check.”

“We did.”

“We checked one turn.”

“And then what?”

Martin said nothing.

Eli waited.

“Another hall?” he said. “Another door? Another floor? You knock again if nothing happens?”

Martin’s jaw tightened.

Eli kept his voice low.

“That’s what I was doing.”

Martin looked at him.

Martin’s eyes dropped to the camera at Eli’s side.

Eli raised the camera slightly, then thought better of it and let it hang at his side.

“We got it.”

“Got what?”

“The sequence.”

“That’s not why I’m still standing here.”

“I know.”

Martin stopped rubbing his thumb against his forefinger.

Behind them, the staff boundary remained closed.

Nothing knocked again.

Martin looked toward it for several seconds.

Then he turned back to Eli.

“You really want to stop now.”

“Yes.”

“After that.”

“Yes.”

Martin rubbed his thumb against his forefinger again.

Eli waited.

At last Martin nodded.

“Okay.”

Eli did not move immediately.

“You mean it?”

“I said okay.”

Martin started back toward the Rider Room.

Eli fell into step beside him.

They had gone perhaps ten feet when Martin glanced over his shoulder.

Eli saw it.

“Don’t.”

Martin faced forward again.

They kept walking.

Neither man answered the last sequence.

